---
import ToolLayout from "../../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro";
import { DateDifferenceCalculator } from "../../../components/calculators/DateDifferenceCalculator.js";
import { getToolByPath } from "../../../content/tools.js";

const tool = getToolByPath("/tools/calculators/date-difference")!;
---

<ToolLayout
  tool={tool}
  purpose="Count the days elapsed from the start date to the end date. 1 January to 2 January is 1 day, and the same date on both sides is 0 days."
>
  <DateDifferenceCalculator client:load />

  <Fragment slot="details">
    <h2>Are the start and end dates included?</h2>
    <p>
      No. The result is the number of days from the start date up to the end date, the same way a hotel stay counts
      nights between check-in and check-out. 1 January to 2 January is 1 day. Entering the same date twice is 0 days.
      Add 1 to the total when you need both the start date and the end date included.
    </p>

    <h2>Worked example</h2>
    <p>
      The calculator opens on 1 January 2024 to 31 December 2024. That span is 365 days, shown as 52 weeks and 1 day,
      or 0 years, 11 months, and 30 days. 2024 includes 29 February, and that day is inside the span. Counting both
      endpoints as well makes it 366 days.
    </p>

    <h2>Assumptions and limitations</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>If the end date is earlier than the start date, the tool still works — it swaps them before calculating, so the result is always a positive difference.</li>
      <li>Dates are compared as calendar dates only, with no time zone applied, so results don't depend on your location.</li>
      <li>The years/months/days breakdown uses calendar arithmetic (e.g. "1 month" can represent 28–31 days depending on which months are spanned).</li>
    </ul>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
